DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaammsspplliitt reads a PNM or PAM stream as input.  It copies each  image  in
       the input into a separate file, in the same format.

       _n_e_t_p_b_m_f_i_l_e  is  the file name of the input file, or -- to indicate Stan‐
       dard Input.  The default is Standard Input.

       _o_u_t_p_u_t___f_i_l_e___p_a_t_t_e_r_n tells how to name the output files.  It is the file
       name of the output file, except that the first occurrence of "%d" in it
       is replaced by the image sequence number  in  unpadded  ASCII  decimal,
       with  the  sequence starting at 0.  If there is no "%d" in the pattern,
       ppaammsspplliitt fails.

       The default output file pattern is "image%d".

       The --ppaaddnnaammee option specifies how many characters you  want  the  image
       sequence  number  in the output file name padded with zeroes.  ppaammsspplliitt
       adds leading zeroes to the image sequence number to get up to at  least
       that  number  of  characters.  This is just the number of characters in
       the sequence number part of the name.  For example, pamsplit -  output‐
       file%d.ppm  -padname=3  would yield output filenames oouuttppuuttffiillee000000..ppppmm,
       oouuttppuuttffiillee000011..ppppmm, etc.

       Note that to do the reverse operation (combining multiple  single-image
       Netpbm  files  into a multi-image one), there is no special Netpbm pro‐
       gram.  Just use ccaatt.

       If you just want to find out basic information about the  images  in  a
       stream, you can use ppaammffiillee on the stream.

       To extract images from a stream and generate a single stream containing
       them, use ppaammppiicckk.

       To run a program on each image in a stream without the hassle of tempo‐
       rary files, use ppaammeexxeecc.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppaammsspplliitt  recognizes  the
       following command line option:



       --ppaaddnnaammee==_n
              Specify  the width (i.e. number of digits) of the image sequence
              number field in the filenames of the output  files.   The  image
              sequence  will  be  padded  with  leading  zeroes to achieve the
              stated width.

              The default is no padding (equivalent to --ppaaddnnaammee==00).

              The --ppaaddnnaammee option was new in Netpbm 10.23 (July 2004).  Before
              that, there was never any padding.




